# Timer / delayed-lambda QObject safety

A raw `T*` captured by a `QTimer` (or queued) lambda is not nulled when the object is destroyed. `if (!button) return;` does not prevent use-after-free. A `QHash<QWidget*, QTimer*>` also dangles if the widget dies while the map still lives (follow-up buttons, `deleteLater`).

- Parent the timer to the widget so it dies with it. Do not keep a parallel raw timer map.
- `connect(..., context, lambda)` with the **widget** as context so Qt drops the slot before destruction.
- Capture `QPointer<T>` (or use a `QPointer` parameter) and return if it is null.
- `destroyed()` may clear observer maps; it is not a substitute for `QPointer` + context.

```cpp
// BAD — raw capture; null check is a no-op after delete; map dangles
connect(timer, &QTimer::timeout, this, [button]() {
  if (!button) return;
  button->setProperty("flash", false);
});
flashTimers_.insert(button, timer);

// GOOD — timer is a child; context is the button; QPointer observes
timer = new QTimer(button);
connect(timer, &QTimer::timeout, button,
        [buttonGuard = QPointer<QPushButton>(button)]() {
          if (!buttonGuard) return;
          buttonGuard->setProperty("flash", false);
        });
```
